<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Unitarianism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Unitarianism is a nontrinitarian movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> that affirms the unitary nature of God and rejects the doctrine of the Trinity; most Unitarians respect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> as a spiritual and moral teacher, and many as savior, but not as equal to God. It arose in the mid-16th century among the Polish Brethren in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and in the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, where Ferenc Dávid founded the Unitarian Church of Transylvania. Unitarians faced repression in 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where the first Unitarian church was established on Essex Street, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in 1774. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, King's Chapel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> accepted the faith from 1784.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Church membership was low because it had not kept up with population growth, and Enlightenment rationalism was leading many people towards atheism, Deism, <b>Unitarianism</b> and Universalism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Background</a></li></ul></section>
